integrated_rules:
Given a claim that a technology (e.g., Internet engagement tools) is making people “dumber,” the conclusion should follow this conditional test: 

**Conclude “the technology makes people dumber”** only if the argument shows (i) the *default* user experience systematically steers attention and reward toward faster, low-effort novelty in a way that predictably reduces practice of patience-and-concentration-intensive cognition (i.e., it changes the difficulty/reward schedule so users accumulate less training handling ambiguity and sustained comprehension), **and** (ii) the argument provides population-level evidence (or strong quasi-experimental indicators such as IQ/test-score trends tightly aligned to expansion, treated as an indicator not sole proof) that *net outcomes* on relevant cognitive capacities/real-world skills decline rather than being offset by tradeoffs (e.g., greater learning volume via easier access/search, externalized memory, or compensatory deep-work opportunities); 

**Conclude “insufficient basis for the universal ‘makes us stupid’ claim / effects are mixed or redirected rather than net decline”** if the argument fails either (i) to establish that the dominant engagement design reliably reduces the relevant cognitive practice in ordinary use (distinguishing “preference for speed” from reduced intelligence and accounting for non-uniform exposure), or (ii) to establish that any attention/shallowing harms produce a consistent net decline at the population level that outweighs plausible compensating gains (including benefits to learning/access for subgroups).
